"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkmy_docusaurus=globalThis.webpackChunkmy_docusaurus||[]).push([[9724],{6048(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"understanding-java-platform","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/understanding-java-platform","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-11-02-tim-hieu-ve-Java.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-tim-hieu-ve-Java.md","title":"Understanding the Java Platform \u2013 Execution Flow & JVM Architecture","description":"1. Java Platform Overview","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"java","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/java"},{"inline":true,"label":"jvm","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/jvm"},{"inline":true,"label":"bytecode","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/bytecode"},{"inline":true,"label":"jdk","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/jdk"},{"inline":true,"label":"jre","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/jre"},{"inline":true,"label":"jit","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/jit"},{"inline":true,"label":"execution-flow","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/execution-flow"}],"readingTime":4.31,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Mai Duc Anh","title":"Blogger & Developer","url":"https://github.com/maiducanha1anh","imageURL":"https://github.com/yourusername.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"understanding-java-platform","title":"Understanding the Java Platform \u2013 Execution Flow & JVM Architecture","authors":["admin"],"tags":["java","jvm","bytecode","jdk","jre","jit","execution-flow"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Analyzing the JVM Execution Mechanism Through a Concrete Code Example","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/jvm-analysis"}},"content":"## 1. Java Platform Overview\\r\\n\\r\\nJava is not merely **a programming language** \u2014 it is a **complete platform (the Java Platform)**.\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 When we talk about *\\"programming in Java\\"*, we are actually working with **3 core components**:\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Java Language**\\r\\n- **JVM (Java Virtual Machine)**\\r\\n- **Library and tooling ecosystem**\\r\\n\\r\\n### Core Design Goal of Java\\r\\n\\r\\n> **Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)**\\r\\n\\r\\nWrite your code **once**, and run it on:\\r\\n- Windows\\r\\n- Linux\\r\\n- macOS\\r\\n\\r\\n\u2026 **without modifying the code**.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. The Role of JDK, JRE, and JVM\\r\\n\\r\\nThese three concepts are a common source of confusion for Java learners \u2014 so let\'s clarify them definitively.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2.1 JVM \u2013 Java Virtual Machine\\r\\n\\r\\n**What is the JVM?**\\r\\n\\r\\n- The JVM is a **virtual machine**\\r\\n- It executes **Java bytecode**\\r\\n- It acts as an **intermediary layer between Java and the operating system**\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc The JVM:\\r\\n- Does not understand `.java` files\\r\\n- Does not understand `.exe` files\\r\\n- **Only understands `.class` files (bytecode)**\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 Each operating system has its **own JVM implementation**, but:\\r\\n- The bytecode itself is **identical across all platforms**\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2.2 JRE \u2013 Java Runtime Environment\\r\\n\\r\\n**The JRE consists of:**\\r\\n\\r\\n- JVM\\r\\n- Core Java Libraries (`java.lang`, `java.util`, ...)\\r\\n- Runtime configuration files\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 The JRE is used to:\\r\\n- **Run Java programs**\\r\\n- \u274c Not for writing or compiling code\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc With only the JRE installed:\\r\\n- You can run `.class` files\\r\\n- \u274c You cannot compile `.java` files\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2.3 JDK \u2013 Java Development Kit\\r\\n\\r\\n**JDK = JRE + Development Tools**\\r\\n\\r\\nIncludes:\\r\\n- `javac` (compiler)\\r\\n- `java` (launcher)\\r\\n- `javadoc`, `jdb`, ...\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 The JDK is used for:\\r\\n- Writing code\\r\\n- Compiling\\r\\n- Running\\r\\n- Debugging\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc When installing Java for development purposes:\\r\\n\u27a1\ufe0f **Always install the JDK**\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### 2.4 Overall Relationship\\r\\nJDK <br />\\r\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 JRE <br />\\r\\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 JVM <br />\\r\\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Java Core Libraries <br />\\r\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 Development Tools <br />\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Relationship Between Java Language and the Java Platform\\r\\n\\r\\n### Java Language\\r\\n- Syntax and keywords\\r\\n- Data types\\r\\n- OOP concepts (classes, objects, inheritance)\\r\\n- Source files with `.java` extension\\r\\n\\r\\n### Java Platform\\r\\n- JVM (Java Virtual Machine)\\r\\n- ClassLoader Subsystem\\r\\n- Runtime Data Areas\\r\\n- Execution Engine & Garbage Collector\\r\\n\\r\\n**The Java Language is only the visible surface.**  \\r\\n**The Java Platform is what ultimately determines how a program executes.**\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Java Execution Flow\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen you run a Java program, **the JVM does not execute the `.java` file directly**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### General Execution Flow\\r\\n\\r\\nIDE  <br />\\r\\n\u2193 (Save)  <br />\\r\\n`Hello.java` (Disk) <br />\\r\\n\u2193 (Compile)  <br />\\r\\n`javac Hello.java`  <br />\\r\\n\u2193  <br />\\r\\n`Hello.class` (Bytecode)  <br />\\r\\n\u2193  <br />\\r\\nJVM (RAM)  <br />\\r\\n\u2193  <br />\\r\\nOS  <br />\\r\\n\u2193  <br />\\r\\nCPU execution  <br />\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. Compile-time and Runtime in Java\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5.1 Compile-time\\r\\n\\r\\n**Triggered when running:**\\r\\n```bash\\r\\njavac Hello.java\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- `.java` file (source code)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Java does:\\r\\n- Validates syntax\\r\\n- Performs type checking\\r\\n- Compiles to bytecode\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\n- `.class` file\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Compile-time does not execute the program.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 5.2 Runtime\\r\\n\\r\\nTriggered when running:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\njava Hello\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- `.class` file\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Java does:\\r\\n- JVM loads the class\\r\\n- Verifies bytecode\\r\\n- Executes the program\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Runtime is when the program actually runs.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 6. What is Bytecode? Why is Java Portable?\\r\\n\\r\\n**What is Bytecode?**\\r\\n- An intermediate representation\\r\\n- Not native machine code\\r\\n- Platform and CPU-independent\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Bytecode:\\r\\n- Runs on the JVM\\r\\n- The JVM translates it further into native machine code\\r\\n\\r\\n**Why does Java run across multiple platforms?**\\r\\n\\r\\nJava Source (.java)<br />\\r\\n        \u2193<br />\\r\\n   Bytecode (.class)<br />\\r\\n        \u2193<br />\\r\\n \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 <br />\\r\\n \u2502 JVM Windows \u2502 \u2192 CPU Windows <br />\\r\\n \u2502 JVM Linux   \u2502 \u2192 CPU Linux <br />\\r\\n \u2502 JVM macOS   \u2502 \u2192 CPU macOS <br />\\r\\n \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 Java does not run directly on the OS.  \\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 The JVM is what runs on the OS.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 7. JVM Architecture\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM consists of 3 major subsystems:\\r\\n- ClassLoader Subsystem\\r\\n- Runtime Data Areas\\r\\n- Execution Engine\\r\\n\\r\\n## 8. ClassLoader Subsystem\\r\\n\\r\\nResponsibilities:\\r\\n- Load `.class` files into the JVM\\r\\n- Verify their validity\\r\\n- Prepare classes for execution\\r\\n\\r\\n### ClassLoader Types in the JVM\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Bootstrap ClassLoader**  \\r\\n  Loads Java\'s core classes (e.g., `java.lang`, `java.util`)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Platform ClassLoader** *(Java 9+)*  \\r\\n  Loads Java Platform libraries (e.g., `java.sql`, `java.xml`)\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Application ClassLoader**  \\r\\n  Loads classes written by the developer (from the classpath)\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Classes are loaded only when required (lazy loading).\\r\\n\\r\\n## 9. Runtime Data Areas \u2013 JVM Memory Regions\\r\\n\\r\\n### 9.1 Method Area\\r\\n\\r\\nStores:\\r\\n- Class metadata\\r\\n- Method information\\r\\n- Static variables\\r\\n\\r\\n### 9.2 Heap\\r\\n\\r\\n- Stores objects\\r\\n- Shared across the entire JVM\\r\\n- Managed by the Garbage Collector\\r\\n\\r\\n### 9.3 Stack\\r\\n\\r\\nEach thread has its own dedicated Stack.\\r\\n\\r\\nStores:\\r\\n- Local variables\\r\\n- Method call information\\r\\n- References\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Stack: small and fast  \\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Heap: larger and relatively slower\\r\\n\\r\\n### 9.4 PC Register\\r\\n\\r\\nStores the address of the currently executing bytecode instruction.\\r\\n\\r\\n### 9.5 Native Method Stack\\r\\n\\r\\nUsed for native code written in C/C++.\\r\\n\\r\\n## 10. Execution Engine\\r\\n\\r\\n### 10.1 Interpreter\\r\\n\\r\\n- Reads bytecode\\r\\n- Executes each instruction sequentially\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Advantage:\\r\\n- Starts execution immediately\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Disadvantage:\\r\\n- Slow for code that is executed repeatedly\\r\\n\\r\\n### 10.2 JIT Compiler (Just-In-Time)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat the JIT does:\\r\\n- Detects frequently executed code paths (hot spots)\\r\\n- Compiles bytecode \u2192 native machine code\\r\\n- Caches the result for reuse\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Modern JVMs use:\\r\\n- **Interpreter + JIT**\\r\\n- The JVM self-optimizes as the program runs\\r\\n\\r\\n## 11. Summary\\r\\n\\r\\nDetailed slide reference:\\r\\n- \ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAHApyPYZmw/AQAchpZL_3B77rpOe4-3YQ/edit\\r\\n\\r\\nJava is not simply a language \u2014 it is a complete platform comprising the JDK, JRE, and JVM. A Java program does not run directly on the operating system; it must first be compiled into bytecode, which is then interpreted and optimized by the JIT compiler inside the JVM before the CPU executes it.\\r\\n\\r\\nA solid understanding of:\\r\\n\\r\\n- The Java Platform <br/>\\r\\n- The Execution Flow <br/>\\r\\n- JVM Architecture <br/>\\r\\n\\r\\nenables developers to:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Gain deep insight into how Java works under the hood <br/>\\r\\n- Debug more effectively <br/>\\r\\n- Write more optimized code <br/>\\r\\n- Build a strong foundation for learning Spring, JVM tuning, and advanced backend development <br/>"},{"id":"jvm-analysis","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/jvm-analysis","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-02-22-phan-tich-JVM.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-22-phan-tich-JVM.md","title":"Analyzing the JVM Execution Mechanism Through a Concrete Code Example","description":"In this post, we will analyze the complete JVM execution mechanism when running the following code:","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"java","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/java"},{"inline":true,"label":"jvm","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/jvm"},{"inline":true,"label":"classloader","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/classloader"},{"inline":true,"label":"runtime-data-areas","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/runtime-data-areas"},{"inline":true,"label":"bytecode","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/bytecode"},{"inline":true,"label":"execution-engine","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/execution-engine"}],"readingTime":9.48,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Mai Duc Anh","title":"Blogger & Developer","url":"https://github.com/maiducanha1anh","imageURL":"https://github.com/yourusername.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"jvm-analysis","title":"Analyzing the JVM Execution Mechanism Through a Concrete Code Example","authors":["admin"],"tags":["java","jvm","classloader","runtime-data-areas","bytecode","execution-engine"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Understanding the Java Platform \u2013 Execution Flow & JVM Architecture","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/understanding-java-platform"},"nextItem":{"title":"Understanding Python \u2013 From Overview to Internal Mechanisms","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/understanding-python"}},"content":"In this post, we will analyze the complete JVM execution mechanism when running the following code:\\r\\n\\r\\n```java\\r\\nclass Student {\\r\\n    int age;\\r\\n}\\r\\n\\r\\npublic class Main {\\r\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\r\\n        Student s1 = new Student();\\r\\n        s1.age = 20;\\r\\n\\r\\n        Student s2 = s1;\\r\\n        s2.age = 25;\\r\\n\\r\\n        int x = 10;\\r\\n        int y = x;\\r\\n\\r\\n        System.out.println(\\"s1.age = \\" + s1.age);\\r\\n        System.out.println(\\"s2.age = \\" + s2.age);\\r\\n        System.out.println(\\"x = \\" + x);\\r\\n        System.out.println(\\"y = \\" + y);\\r\\n    }\\r\\n}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n## I. INPUT AND OUTPUT OF THE JVM\\r\\n\\r\\nINPUT <br />\\r\\nWhen running:\\r\\n``` bash\\r\\njava Main\\r\\n```\\r\\nThe JVM receives as input:\\r\\n- `Main.class` \u2014 the compiled output of `Main.java`\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/Main.cl.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n- `Student.class` \u2014 the compiled output of `Student.java`\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/Student.cl.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n- The bytecode (an intermediate representation produced after compiling `.java` files, which is then executed by the JVM) contained within these files\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/byecode.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n\u26a0\ufe0f Note: The JVM (the virtual machine that executes Java programs) does not run `.java` source code \u2014 it runs the bytecode inside `.class` files.\\r\\n\\r\\nOUTPUT <br />\\r\\n- Console output (the result printed to the screen)\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/daura.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n- Memory state changes\\r\\n- Objects (instances created from a class) allocated in the Heap (the JVM memory region used to store objects)\\r\\n- Stack Frames (a block of data created each time a method is invoked; it resides in the Java Stack of each thread) being pushed and popped\\r\\n\\r\\nConsole output:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\ns1.age = 25\\r\\ns2.age = 25\\r\\nx = 10\\r\\ny = 10\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## II. CLASS LOADER SUBSYSTEM (The JVM component responsible for loading `.class` files into memory and preparing them for execution)\\r\\n\\r\\n**1. Loading** (The process of reading `.class` files and bringing them into JVM memory)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Application ClassLoader loads:\\r\\n\\r\\n- `Main.class`\\r\\n- `Student.class`\\r\\n- Class metadata is stored in the Method Area\\r\\n\\r\\nThe **Method Area** is a memory region used to store information about loaded classes, including:\\r\\n- Method bytecode\\r\\n- Class metadata\\r\\n- Runtime Constant Pool\\r\\n- Static variables\\r\\n\\r\\n**Metadata** is data that describes a class, including:\\r\\n- Class name: `Student`\\r\\n- Superclass: `Object`\\r\\n- List of fields\\r\\n- List of methods\\r\\n- Access modifiers\\r\\n\\r\\n**2. Linking** (A safety verification step)\\r\\n\\r\\nConsists of 3 sub-steps:\\r\\n\\r\\n\u2714 **Verify** (Checks whether the bytecode is valid and safe)\\r\\n\\r\\n- Validates bytecode correctness\\r\\n- Ensures no stack overflow\\r\\n- Ensures no incorrect type access\\r\\n\\r\\n\u2714 **Prepare** (Allocates memory for static variables and assigns default values)\\r\\n\\r\\n- Allocates memory for static fields (if any)\\r\\n- Assigns default values\\r\\n\\r\\n\u2714 **Resolve** (Converts symbolic references into direct references)\\r\\n\\r\\n- Transforms symbolic references \u2192 direct references\\r\\n- A **symbolic reference** is textual information (e.g., a class or method name as a string)\\r\\n- A **direct reference** is the actual memory address of the method at runtime\\r\\n\\r\\n**3. Initialization**\\r\\n\\r\\n- Executes static blocks (a block of code that runs exactly once when the class is first loaded) if present\\r\\n- Initializes static variables (which reside in the Method Area)\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter this phase, the Method Area contains:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\nClass Student\\r\\n  field: age\\r\\n  constructor\\r\\n\\r\\nClass Main\\r\\n  method: main()\\r\\n  bytecode\\r\\n  constant pool\\r\\n  ```\\r\\n**What is the Constant Pool?**\\r\\n- The Constant Pool is a table containing all \\"constants\\" and \\"symbolic references\\" used by the class.\\r\\n- It resides inside the `.class` file.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/byecode.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nA **constructor** is a special method of a class, used to initialize an object when it is created with the `new` keyword.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## III. 16-STEP RUNTIME WALKTHROUGH VIA JAVA VISUALIZER\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 1 \u2014 JVM initializes the process** (a program currently running in the operating system)\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 1](/img/anh1.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhy does it show `main:7`?\\r\\n- The JVM has no concept of \\"line 7\\"\\r\\n- The JVM only understands bytecode indices\\r\\n- `main:7` is displayed by the Java Visualizer, which maps bytecode indices back to corresponding source lines\\r\\n\\r\\nThe PC Register (a small memory region that stores the address of the bytecode instruction currently being executed by the thread) points to the first bytecode instruction of the `main` method. The tool displays this as `main:7` because that is the corresponding source line.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe operating system creates a new PROCESS.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis process contains:\\r\\n- **Heap** (memory region for storing objects and class instances)\\r\\n- **Method Area** (stores class information after loading)\\r\\n- **Stack** (a thread component that stores local variables, references to objects, the operand stack, and method call information)\\r\\n- **PC Register** (a small memory region storing the address of the bytecode instruction currently being executed)\\r\\n- **Native Method Stack** (used when Java calls native methods written in C/C++)\\r\\n\\r\\nThese collectively form the **JVM Runtime Data Areas**.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe ClassLoader begins operating.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM does not immediately run `main`. It must first:\\r\\n- Load class `Main`\\r\\n- Load class `Student`\\r\\n\\r\\nMechanism:\\r\\nThe Application ClassLoader will:\\r\\n- Read `Main.class`\\r\\n- Load its bytecode into the Method Area\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 2 \u2014 Create the Main Thread**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 2](/img/anh2.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM creates:\\r\\n\\r\\n- **Main Thread** (the first thread created when a Java program starts)\\r\\n- **Stack** (each thread has its own dedicated Stack; the JVM creates a Stack Frame)\\r\\n- **Frame main** (the Stack Frame created when `main()` is invoked)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen the Main Thread is created, the JVM allocates a dedicated Stack for it and pushes a stack frame for `main()` onto the Stack. This frame contains an empty local variable array and an empty operand stack. The PC Register is set to point to the first bytecode instruction of `main` (the Visualizer displays this as the corresponding source line number, but the JVM internally only tracks the bytecode index).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe **operand stack** is a memory region inside each Stack Frame used to store operands and intermediate results during bytecode execution.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 2](/img/buoc2.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 3 \u2014 `new Student()`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 3](/img/anh3.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM encounters `new Student()`:\\r\\n- Allocates a new object in the Heap\\r\\n- The field `age` is assigned the default value `0`\\r\\n\\r\\nStack at this point:\\r\\n- Still only contains `frame main()`\\r\\n\\r\\n`<init>` is the special bytecode name for constructors.\\r\\nInside the `<init>` frame, the first local variable is always `this`.\\r\\n`this` is a reference to the object currently being initialized.\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen executing `Student s1 = new Student()`, the JVM sees the bytecode instruction to create a new object \u2014 it allocates memory in the Heap for a `Student` with a default `age` of `0`, then pushes a reference to this object onto the operand stack of `frame main()`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 3](/img/buoc3.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 4 \u2014 Invoke the constructor**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 4](/img/anh4.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM invokes the `<init>` constructor by creating a new stack frame for `<init>` and pushing it onto the Stack. The `<init>` frame contains a first local variable `this`, which is a reference pointing to the newly created object in the Heap \u2014 this tells the constructor which object is being initialized.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 4](/img/buoc4.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 5 \u2014 Constructor returns**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 5](/img/anh5.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nOnce the constructor completes, the `<init>` frame is popped off the Stack, returning control to `frame main`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 5](/img/buoc5.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 6 \u2014 Assign to `s1`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 6](/img/anh6.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nInside `frame main()`, the JVM takes the reference returned by the constructor and stores it in the local variable slot for `s1`. At this point, `frame main()` in the Stack contains an entry `s1` holding a reference that points to a `Student` object in the Heap, where `age = 0`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 6](/img/buoc6.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 7 \u2014 `s1.age = 20`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 7](/img/anh7.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat the JVM does:\\r\\n- Retrieves the reference `s1` from the Stack\\r\\n- Follows the address into the Heap\\r\\n- Locates the field `age`\\r\\n- Updates the value to `20`\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen the JVM sees `s1.age = 20`, it retrieves the reference from `s1` in the Stack, follows the address into the Heap, finds the `age` field of the object, and sets it to `20`. The Heap now contains a `Student` object with `age = 20`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 7](/img/buoc7.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 8 \u2014 `Student s2 = s1`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 8](/img/anh8.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM does NOT create a new object.\\r\\n\\r\\nIt only:\\r\\n- Copies the reference value from `s1`\\r\\n- Assigns it to `s2`\\r\\n\\r\\n`Student s2 = s1` does not allocate a new object \u2014 it simply copies the reference value from `s1` to `s2` inside `frame main()`. As a result, both `s1` and `s2` in the Stack point to the same object in the Heap.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 8](/img/buoc8.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n=> `s1` and `s2` reference the same object.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 9 \u2014 `s2.age = 25`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 9](/img/anh9.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM:\\r\\n- Retrieves the reference from `s2`\\r\\n- Follows it to the object in the Heap\\r\\n- Updates `age = 25`\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen executing `s2.age = 25`, the JVM accesses the object in the Heap via the reference stored in `s2` and updates the `age` field to `25`. Since both references point to the same object, both `s1.age` and `s2.age` now reflect the value `25`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 9](/img/buoc9.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 10 \u2014 `int x = 10`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 10](/img/anh10.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen the JVM encounters `int x = 10`, it stores the integer value `10` directly into a slot in the local variable array of `frame main()`.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 10](/img/buoc10.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Heap is unchanged.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 11 \u2014 `int y = x`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 11](/img/anh11.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n`int y = x` copies the value `10` from `x` to `y`. Both `x` and `y` are now separate integer variables in the Stack, each holding the value `10`, with no involvement of the Heap.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 11](/img/buoc11.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nNo references are involved here.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 12 \u2014 Preparing the first `println`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 12](/img/anh12.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat the JVM does:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Retrieves data from the Stack or Heap\\r\\n- Pushes it onto the operand stack\\r\\n- Invokes `println()`\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen processing `System.out.println` calls, the JVM sequentially retrieves the required values from the Stack or accesses fields in the Heap via references, pushes them onto the operand stack, and invokes the `println()` methods.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 12](/img/buoc12.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 13 \u2014 Invoke `println()`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 13](/img/anh13.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nSame process as Step 12.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 13](/img/buoc13.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 14 \u2014 Print `x`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 14](/img/anh14.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nSame process as Step 12.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 14](/img/buoc14.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 15 \u2014 Print `y`**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 15](/img/anh15.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nSame process as Step 12.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 15](/img/buoc15.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**STEP 16 \u2014 `main()` completes**\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 16](/img/anh16.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nStack state during `println` calls:\\r\\n```\\r\\nFrame println()\\r\\nFrame main()\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter each print completes:\\r\\n- `Frame println()` is popped.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe JVM creates temporary stack frames for each method call and pops them after execution completes.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Stack is now empty.\\r\\n\\r\\n![Step 16](/img/buoc16.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe object in the Heap has no remaining references.\\r\\n\\r\\n- The Garbage Collector will reclaim it in a future collection cycle.\\r\\n\\r\\nFinally, when `main()` completes, the JVM pops `frame main()` off the Stack, leaving the Stack empty. The `Student` object in the Heap now has no references pointing to it and is therefore eligible for garbage collection. The Heap retains the object until the GC runs and reclaims it.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## IV. MEMORY SUMMARY\\r\\n\\r\\n| Component     | Stores                                      |\\r\\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------|\\r\\n| Method Area   | Metadata, bytecode, constant pool           |\\r\\n| Heap          | Objects                                     |\\r\\n| Stack         | Primitives + References                     |\\r\\n| PC Register   | Current bytecode instruction address        |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## V. Conclusion\\r\\n\\r\\nDetailed slide reference:\\r\\n- \ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAHBYC9gqew/Qj0cc0miT3og0gFKV8UZJw/edit\\r\\n\\r\\nCore takeaways:\\r\\n- Objects always reside in the Heap\\r\\n- References reside in the Stack\\r\\n- Primitives reside in the Stack\\r\\n- A method call creates a new Stack Frame\\r\\n- The Constant Pool stores symbolic references\\r\\n- The Execution Engine reads and interprets bytecode\\r\\n- The GC reclaims objects when no references remain\\r\\n\\r\\nThrough this simple example, we can see that the JVM does not merely \\"run code\\" \u2014 it:\\r\\n- Loads classes\\r\\n- Verifies bytecode\\r\\n- Manages memory\\r\\n- Executes via a stack-based architecture\\r\\n- Invokes native code\\r\\n- Automatically performs garbage collection\\r\\n\\r\\nA solid understanding of these mechanisms enables developers to:\\r\\n- Avoid memory leaks\\r\\n- Clearly distinguish references from primitives\\r\\n- Understand why and how GC operates\\r\\n- Debug JVM-related issues at a deeper level"},{"id":"understanding-python","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/understanding-python","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-02-02-tim-hieu-ve-Python.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-02-tim-hieu-ve-Python.md","title":"Understanding Python \u2013 From Overview to Internal Mechanisms","description":"1. General Overview of Python","date":"2026-02-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"python","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/python"},{"inline":true,"label":"interpreted","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/interpreted"},{"inline":true,"label":"compiled","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/compiled"},{"inline":true,"label":"cpython","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/cpython"},{"inline":true,"label":"memory-management","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/memory-management"}],"readingTime":4.81,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Mai Duc Anh","title":"Blogger & Developer","url":"https://github.com/maiducanha1anh","imageURL":"https://github.com/yourusername.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"understanding-python","sidebar_position":1,"title":"Understanding Python \u2013 From Overview to Internal Mechanisms","authors":["admin"],"tags":["python","interpreted","compiled","cpython","memory-management"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Analyzing the JVM Execution Mechanism Through a Concrete Code Example","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/jvm-analysis"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Guide to Building a Personal Blog with Docusaurus","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/first-post"}},"content":"## 1. General Overview of Python\\r\\n\\r\\nPython is a high-level programming language created by **Guido van Rossum**, first officially released in **1991**. Python was designed with the philosophy of being **simple, readable, easy to learn, and easy to maintain**, allowing developers to focus more on business logic rather than complex syntax.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Key Features of Python\\r\\n\\r\\n- Syntax close to natural language, highly readable\\r\\n- **Multi-purpose language**: Web, AI, Data Science, Automation, Game development, IoT\\r\\n- Rich ecosystem of libraries (NumPy, Pandas, TensorFlow, Django, Flask, ...)\\r\\n- Cross-platform: Windows, Linux, macOS\\r\\n- Large community, extensive documentation\\r\\n\\r\\nPython is especially well-suited for:\\r\\n- IT students\\r\\n- Beginners in programming\\r\\n- Systems that require rapid development\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Interpreted vs Compiled \u2013 Execution Mechanisms\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen discussing how a language is executed, we typically distinguish between two concepts: **Compiled** and **Interpreted**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Compiled Language\\r\\n\\r\\nExamples: C, C++, Go\\r\\n\\r\\n**Workflow:**\\r\\n\\r\\nSource Code (.c) <br />\\r\\n\u2193 <br />\\r\\nCompiler <br />\\r\\n\u2193 <br />\\r\\nMachine Code (.exe) <br />\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCharacteristics:\\r\\n- Compiles the **entire** program before execution\\r\\n- Fast execution, high performance\\r\\n- Platform and CPU architecture dependent\\r\\n- Harder to debug\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n### Interpreted Language\\r\\n\\r\\nExamples: Python, JavaScript, PHP\\r\\n\\r\\nWorkflow:\\r\\n\\r\\nSource Code (.py) <br />\\r\\n\u2193 <br />\\r\\nInterpreter <br />\\r\\n\u2193 <br />\\r\\nLine-by-line execution <br />\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCharacteristics:\\r\\n- No need to produce an `.exe` file\\r\\n- Easier to debug, easier to learn\\r\\n- Generally slower than compiled languages\\r\\n\\r\\n\u26a0\ufe0f **Python is not purely interpreted** \u2013 this will become clearer when we look at CPython.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Python\'s Execution Workflow \u2013 From Source Code to Running Program\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen you run a Python file (`file.py`), Python does not execute the source code directly.\\r\\n\\r\\n### General Workflow\\r\\n\\r\\nIDE <br />\\r\\n \u2193 (Save) <br />\\r\\nfile.py (Disk) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 (Run) <br />\\r\\nSource Code (RAM) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nLexical Analysis \u2192 Tokens (RAM) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nParsing \u2192 AST (RAM) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nCompile <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nBytecode (RAM + optionally written as .pyc to disk) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nPython Virtual Machine (RAM) <br />\\r\\n \u2193 <br />\\r\\nCPU execution <br />\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n### Detailed Breakdown\\r\\n\\r\\n**Stage 1: Python reads file.py into memory**\\r\\n(Python Source Code \u2192 RAM)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen you run:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\npython main.py\\r\\n```\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- file.py\\r\\n- Location: disk\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Python does:\\r\\n- The OS reads the contents of file.py\\r\\n- Loads the entire source code into RAM\\r\\n- Converts code into a character string (text)\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\n- Python source code resides in RAM\\r\\n- file.py remains on disk, unmodified\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc From this point on:\\r\\n\\r\\nPython only operates on the code in RAM\\r\\n\\r\\nThe disk serves only as storage\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**Stage 2: Lexical Analysis & Parsing**\\r\\n(Source Code \u2192 AST)\\r\\n\\r\\n2.1 Lexical Analysis\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- Source code (text) in RAM\\r\\n\\r\\nExample:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\na = 10\\r\\n```\\r\\nWhat Python does:\\r\\n\\r\\nBreaks code into tokens:\\r\\n\\r\\na \u2192 IDENTIFIER\\r\\n\\r\\n= \u2192 OPERATOR\\r\\n\\r\\n10 \u2192 NUMBER\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc At this stage:\\r\\n\\r\\nPython does not yet understand the logic\\r\\n\\r\\nIt only recognizes \\"symbols\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n2.2 Parsing\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- Token stream (RAM)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Python does:\\r\\n- Validates syntax correctness\\r\\n- Builds an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST)\\r\\n\\r\\nExample:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\na = 10\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n\u2192 AST represents:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Assign value 10 to variable a\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\n- AST\\r\\n- Resides in RAM\\r\\n\\r\\n\u274c If syntax is invalid \u2192 SyntaxError (execution stops here)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**Stage 3: Compilation to Bytecode**\\r\\n(AST \u2192 Bytecode .pyc)\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- AST (RAM)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Python does:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Compiles the AST into Bytecode\\r\\n\\r\\nBytecode is:\\r\\n\\r\\n- An intermediate representation\\r\\n\\r\\n- Not machine code\\r\\n\\r\\n- Platform-independent\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Bytecode\\r\\n\\r\\n- Resides in RAM\\r\\n\\r\\nAdditionally:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Python may write bytecode to disk\\r\\n\\r\\n- As a .pyc file inside the `__pycache__` directory\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Important:\\r\\n\\r\\n.pyc is not always generated\\r\\n\\r\\nIt is used to:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Speed up subsequent runs\\r\\n\\r\\n- Avoid recompilation if the source code hasn\'t changed\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n**Stage 4: Python Virtual Machine (PVM)**\\r\\n(Bytecode \u2192 Execution)\\r\\n\\r\\nInput:\\r\\n- Bytecode (RAM)\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat Python does:\\r\\n\\r\\n+ Python Virtual Machine:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Reads each instruction in the bytecode\\r\\n\\r\\n- Executes them sequentially\\r\\n\\r\\n+ Each instruction:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Calls the corresponding C function in CPython\\r\\n\\r\\n- The CPU executes the real instruction\\r\\n\\r\\nOutput:\\r\\n\\r\\n- The program runs\\r\\n\\r\\n- Creates variables and objects in the heap\\r\\n\\r\\n- Prints to screen, writes files, calls APIs, etc.\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udccc Critical note:\\r\\n\\r\\n- PVM runs in RAM\\r\\n\\r\\n- The CPU does not execute Python source code\\r\\n\\r\\n- The CPU only executes the machine code of CPython\\r\\n\\r\\n\u27a1\ufe0f This is why Python is commonly described as:\\r\\n\\r\\n> **\\"An interpreted language with a compilation step\\"**\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. What is CPython and Why Does It Matter?\\r\\n\\r\\n### CPython\\r\\n\\r\\nCPython is the **most widely used implementation of Python**, written in **C**.\\r\\n\\r\\n\ud83d\udc49 When you download Python from `python.org`, you are using **CPython**.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Role of CPython\\r\\n\\r\\n- Compiles Python code \u2192 Bytecode\\r\\n- Executes bytecode via the PVM\\r\\n- Manages memory\\r\\n- Provides an API for C Extensions\\r\\n\\r\\n### Other Python Implementations\\r\\n\\r\\n- **PyPy**: uses JIT compilation, can be faster than CPython\\r\\n- **Jython**: runs on the JVM\\r\\n- **IronPython**: runs on .NET\\r\\n\\r\\n\u27a1\ufe0f However, **CPython remains the de facto standard**.\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. How Does Python Manage Memory?\\r\\n\\r\\nPython **manages memory automatically**; developers do not need to manually allocate or free memory.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Main Memory Regions\\r\\n\\r\\n1. **Stack Memory**\\r\\n   - Stores local variables\\r\\n   - Manages function calls\\r\\n\\r\\n2. **Heap Memory**\\r\\n   - Stores objects (list, dict, class instances, \u2026)\\r\\n   - Managed by CPython\\r\\n\\r\\n### Every Python Object Consists of\\r\\n\\r\\n- Value\\r\\n- Data type\\r\\n- Reference count (number of active references)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 6. Garbage Collection in Python\\r\\n\\r\\n### Reference Counting\\r\\n\\r\\nPython uses **reference counting** as its primary memory management mechanism.\\r\\n\\r\\n- Each object tracks its reference count\\r\\n- When the count reaches 0 \u2192 the object is deallocated\\r\\n\\r\\nExample:\\r\\n```python\\r\\na = []\\r\\nb = a\\r\\n\\r\\ndel a\\r\\ndel b  # only at this point is the list object actually freed\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n## 7. Summary\\r\\n\\r\\nSlide reference:\\r\\n- \ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAG_3zWkweQ/ZLs9aa_VVotSC-yXgsAZOw/edit\\r\\n\\r\\nPython is a language with clean syntax that is easy to learn, yet **internally operates through a relatively complex set of mechanisms**. When running a Python program, source code is not executed directly \u2014 it goes through multiple stages including lexical analysis, parsing, compilation to bytecode, and execution via the **Python Virtual Machine (PVM)**.\\r\\n\\r\\nPython is not a purely interpreted language; it is better described as an **interpreted language with an intermediate compilation step**. **CPython** plays a central role in compilation, execution, and memory management. Thanks to **automatic memory management and Garbage Collection**, Python lets developers focus on logic rather than resource handling.\\r\\n\\r\\nUnderstanding Python\'s internal workflow and mechanisms enables developers to write more efficient code, debug more effectively, and build a solid foundation for working with Python in large-scale systems."},{"id":"first-post","metadata":{"permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/first-post","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-01-22-huong-dan-docusaurus.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-22-huong-dan-docusaurus.md","title":"A Guide to Building a Personal Blog with Docusaurus","description":"In this post, I will walk through the detailed steps for creating a personal blog using Docusaurus \u2013 a powerful framework for building documentation websites and static blogs.","date":"2026-01-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docusaurus","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/docusaurus","description":"Docusaurus tag description"},{"inline":true,"label":"blog","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"guide","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/guide"},{"inline":true,"label":"programming","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/tags/programming"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Mai Duc Anh","title":"Blogger & Developer","url":"https://github.com/maiducanha1anh","imageURL":"https://github.com/yourusername.png","key":"admin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"first-post","title":"A Guide to Building a Personal Blog with Docusaurus","authors":["admin"],"tags":["docusaurus","blog","guide","programming"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Understanding Python \u2013 From Overview to Internal Mechanisms","permalink":"/my-docusaurus/blog/understanding-python"}},"content":"In this post, I will walk through the detailed steps for creating a personal blog using **Docusaurus** \u2013 a powerful framework for building documentation websites and static blogs.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis post is intended for:\\r\\n- IT students\\r\\n- Web development beginners\\r\\n- Anyone who wants a professional personal blog\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. What is Docusaurus?\\r\\n\\r\\nDocusaurus is an open-source framework developed by Facebook, designed for creating:\\r\\n- Personal blogs\\r\\n- Documentation websites\\r\\n- Project showcase websites\\r\\n\\r\\nKey advantages:\\r\\n- Free and open-source\\r\\n- Easy to use\\r\\n- Write content in Markdown\\r\\n- Straightforward deployment to GitHub Pages\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Environment Setup\\r\\n\\r\\nPrerequisites:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Node.js (version >= 18)\\r\\n- npm (bundled with Node.js)\\r\\n\\r\\nVerify your installation with:\\r\\n```bash\\r\\nnode -v\\r\\nnpm -v\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n## 4. Steps to Build a Blog with Docusaurus\\r\\n\\r\\nThe slide below illustrates the complete workflow for building and deploying the website,\\r\\nfrom project initialization all the way to deploying on GitHub Pages.\\r\\n\\r\\nView the detailed step-by-step slide here:\\r\\n- \ud83d\udd17 https://www.canva.com/design/DAG-x-HN2J0/AXmxIe_VDDip6aalEPP8PQ/view\\r\\n\\r\\n(The slide will open in a new tab for a clearer, full-screen view)\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n## 5. Conclusion\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter reviewing the slide, beginners should be able to:\\r\\n\\r\\n- Initialize a Docusaurus project\\r\\n- Write and publish blog posts\\r\\n- Push source code to GitHub\\r\\n- Deploy the website using GitHub Pages\\r\\n\\r\\n---"}]}}')}}]);